         <p>Every discovered vertex is reachable, because it is discovered from a discovered vertex by an edge, starting from \(s\). Conversely, a reachable vertex is discovered: induct on \(\delta(v)\). If \(\delta(v) = 0\), then \(v = s\). If \(\delta(v) = k \ge 1\), a shortest path to \(v\) has a vertex \(u\) before \(v\) with \(\delta(u) = k - 1\), which is discovered by induction. BFS removes every discovered vertex, so \(u\) is removed, and when it is, \(v\) is either undiscovered, and becomes discovered, or already discovered.
 
