+        Next, \(\mathrm{dist}(v) \ge \delta(v)\) for every discovered \(v\). The distance of \(v\) is the length of the tree path from \(s\) to \(v\) through parents, which is a walk of that length. Every walk from \(s\) to \(v\) contains a path from \(s\) to \(v\) with no more edges, so this length is at least \(\delta(v)\).
 
         Finally, \(\mathrm{dist}(v) \le \delta(v)\), by induction on \(k = \delta(v)\). For \(k = 0\), \(\mathrm{dist}(s) = 0\). For \(k \ge 1\), take \(u\) as above, with \(\mathrm{dist}(u) = \delta(u) = k - 1\) by induction. If \(v\) was discovered before \(u\) was removed, by a vertex \(w\) removed no later than \(u\), then \(\mathrm{dist}(w) \le \mathrm{dist}(u) = k - 1\) by Lemma 2.1, so \(\mathrm{dist}(v) = \mathrm{dist}(w) + 1 \le k\). Otherwise \(v\) is discovered when \(u\) is removed, and \(\mathrm{dist}(v) = k\). Either way \(\mathrm{dist}(v) \le k\).</p>
