-            <p>In the recursion forest of Definition 3.1, show that the interval of a child call lies strictly inside the interval of its parent.</p>
+            <p>In the recursion forest of the proof of Proposition 3.2, show that the interval of a child call lies strictly inside the interval of its parent.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>The child is discovered during the parent's call, when the parent has been discovered and not yet finished, so its discovery tick is after \(d(\text{parent})\). The child finishes before the parent's loop over neighbours ends, which is before the parent's finish tick \(f(\text{parent})\). So \(d(\text{parent}) &lt; d(\text{child}) &lt; f(\text{child}) &lt; f(\text{parent})\).</p>
             </details>
